{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices (typically +1 to +3 EUR early, widening to +8 to +12 EUR by weeks 3–4 in these samples). Apply a downward level shift to the entire path, increasing with horizon.",
    "Underestimates downside momentum: when history shows a clear downtrend, the model mean-reverts too quickly and fails to project continued declines. Add a trend-following component (negative drift) when the last 1–2 weeks slope is strongly negative.",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: errors compound with lead time; near-term (days 1–5) bias is modest, but beyond ~day 10 the model increasingly overpredicts. Use a horizon ramp correction (e.g., subtract small amount days 1–5, larger amount days 10–30).",
    "Lag in regime shifts: after a break lower, forecasts stay anchored to prior levels and adjust slowly. Introduce a regime-change trigger: if price drops below recent support/MA by a threshold, shift the forecast baseline down immediately.",
    "Over-optimistic rebounds: the model frequently predicts rebounds/plateaus that do not materialize during selloffs. Suppress bounce magnitude during drawdowns (cap positive day-to-day changes until trend stabilizes)."
  ]
}
